  "notes": "An engineer-producer rather than a beatmaker, and the entry says the consequence for the harmony out loud: \"he works as an engineer-producer across a whole album rather than supplying a single beat, so **the harmonic vocabulary follows the artist**\" — static m9 loops, real 4-chord cycles and bossa/jazz-derived turnarounds all appear. So this model's chord vocabulary is the broadest of the twenty producers, carrying a vamp, a cadential cycle and a `ii–V–I` turnaround at real weights rather than one house style. The drums are three states with \"genre-switching inside one album… trap, bossa, punk-adjacent and dembow kits on adjacent tracks\", which is the section's highest `switchUpProb` and a mid-density kit that is neither the atmospheric floor nor the radio ceiling. The 808 is kept because the entry lists it first of three low ends — \"sustained 808 with slides on the trap cuts, played bass on the retro cuts, and a short plucked sub on the Latin cuts\" — on the straight 60–130 band, since an 808 does not require a half-time backbeat. \"His contribution is comping, editing, layering and mixing the vocal into the bed as a compositional act\" has no schema field; what it produces here is a loose grid and an arrangement built as an album suite with segues and short forms. ⚠ `confidence` is **low**, mirroring the entry's medium-low: \"his engineer-producer role and album credits are sourced; parametric detail is inference\". ⚠ Every number here is **INFERRED**, `bpm.mode` included. ⚠ **`session.keys` is INFERRED.** The entry's Keys line describes the harmonic grammar but names no key centre, so these values and their weights are an authored choice — no key in this set is stated in the research.",
